# ============================================================================
# STRATAAI .GITIGNORE
# ============================================================================
# This file tells Git which files and folders to IGNORE (never track/upload).
# Why? Some files are:
#   - Generated (can be recreated, e.g., .venv, __pycache__)
#   - Secret (API keys in .env would be exposed publicly on GitHub)
#   - Large (ChromaDB databases, uploaded PDFs don't belong in Git)
#   - OS-specific (Windows thumbs.db, macOS .DS_Store)
# ============================================================================

# --- Python ---
# __pycache__ contains compiled bytecode (.pyc files) that Python generates
# automatically for performance. They're machine-specific and regenerated on every run.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class

# --- Virtual Environment ---
# The .venv folder contains ~200MB+ of installed packages.
# It's OS-specific and can be perfectly recreated from requirements.txt.
# NEVER commit this — it would bloat your repo and break on other machines.
.venv/
venv/
env/

# --- Environment Variables ---
# .env contains YOUR secret API keys (Gemini, Groq, etc.).
# If committed, anyone who sees your GitHub repo can steal your keys
# and run up charges on your accounts. This is the #1 security mistake beginners make.
.env

# --- IDE Settings ---
# VS Code stores workspace-specific settings here.
# These are personal preferences (your font size, your extensions)
# and shouldn't be forced on collaborators.
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo

# --- Data Files ---
# Uploaded PDFs and ChromaDB databases are runtime data, not source code.
# They can be huge (100MB+ PDFs, GB-sized vector DBs).
# Each user/deployment should have their own data.
data/uploads/
data/chroma_db/

# --- OS Generated ---
# Operating systems create hidden metadata files.
# These are useless clutter in a code repository.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# --- Distribution / Build ---
# These are generated when you package Python projects for distribution.
# Not relevant for our Streamlit app, but good practice to exclude.
dist/
build/
*.egg-info/

# --- Jupyter Notebooks ---
# Checkpoint files are auto-saved backups. The actual .ipynb files
# should be tracked, but checkpoints are redundant copies.
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# --- Database ---
# SQLite database files (Week 9: Progress Tracking).
# Each deployment has its own database — it's data, not code.
*.db
*.sqlite3

# --- Personal Learning Notes ---
# Markdown files generated during pair programming sessions.
# These are personal notes, not part of the application code.
strataai_overview.md
folder_structure_explained.md
core_ideas_explained.md
